  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 330 AM CDT Wed Aug 26 2026\n\nWhile a narrow ribbon of warm advective showers/sprinkles \nmanaged to develop briefly in northern Illinois early this \nmorning, insufficient MUCAPE locally has prevented more robust \nshowers or isolated storms from developing. Meanwhile, a cluster\nof thunderstorms in southwest Wisconsin is drifting toward\nnorthern Illinois. While currently near severe limits, these \nstorms have surged well ahead of the cold front and may soon \noutrun the residual elevated instability axis and better shear, \nand gradually weaken as they approach. A lightning threat may \nstill persist into at least portions of northern Illinois this \nmorning, mainly near and north of I-88. Will continue to monitor\ntrends in the event that a stronger storm manages to persist \nthis far south.\n\nThe parent mid-upper low continues to slowly drift southeast \nacross western Ontario and northeast Minnesota early this \nmorning with the attendant surface cold front currently draped \nalong a line from near La Crosse, WI into the western UP of \nMichigan. The low center will drift slowly east southeast across\nLake Superior into southern Ontario through the day. In tandem,\nthe surface cold front will sweep southeast across the area, \nthe timing of which will be a key factor in the thunderstorm \npotential this afternoon.\n\nAdmittedly, the timing and coverage of thunderstorms later \ntoday along with the severe thunderstorm threat remains highly \nuncertain. Hi-res guidance continues to trend later and farther \nsoutheast with thunderstorm initiation along the cold front, \nlikely owing to an apparent disconnect between the surface front\nlocation and the mid-level forcing with the parent shortwave. \nResidual cloud cover this morning and any showers/storms that \npersist into northeast Illinois may also delay destabilization, \nespecially across northern Illinois. However, model soundings \nindicate that the area may still be largely uncapped ahead of \nthe front this afternoon, particularly near and east of I-55. \nSo it may not take much to initiate at least an isolated storm \nor two earlier than guidance suggests as the front moves through.\nIf storms manage to initiate, instability (2000-2500 J/kg \nMLCAPE) and shear (35-45 kt) will be sufficient for supercells \nand multi-cell clusters capable of localized damaging winds and\nhail. The potential for higher storm coverage and greatest \nsevere threat still favors areas mainly south and east of a \nPaxton to Valparaiso line when the mid-level forcing begins to\ncatch up to the surface front, and low-level convergence \nincreases.\n\nA few additional showers and non-severe storms may develop in \nthe wake of the front closer to the mid-level trough axis late \nafternoon and have held onto 20-30% precip chances through 7 PM \nCDT for portions of the Chicago metro near Lake Michigan to \naccount for this.\n\nLooking ahead, a trailing weaker wave rounding the upper trough\nover eastern Canada could support a few sprinkles or showers on\nThursday (15% chance), especially near the lake breeze, but \nexpect mainly dry conditions with seasonably cool temperatures \nin the upper 70s to around 80 and comfortable humidity. Dry \nweather continues through the end of the week as upper ridging \nbriefly expands east into the area Friday into Saturday paired \nwith expansive surface high pressure. The upper levels then \ntransition toward a more active pattern across the northern \nPlains and Upper Midwest as a series of disturbances move atop \nthe upper ridge and across the broader region early next week. \nThis will bring periodic shower and storm chances with \ntemperatures also trend warmer with highs in the mid-upper 80s.\n\nPetr",
